WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.809 --> 00:00:05.600
From rare strains of Kryptonite to the Martian
Manhunter's imaginary friend, to a Gotham

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.170
City franchise opening up a new branch, here
are the Supergirl Easter eggs that were definitely

00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:12.620
put there for the diehards.

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:18.880
Since its debut in 2015, Supergirl has found
an audience by focusing on a more accessible

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:23.080
version of the DC Universe that's easy for
casual viewers to get a handle on.

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:25.860
After all, everybody knows Superman, right?

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:31.160
Here's his cousin, she's got the same powers,
does the same stuff, is a similarly good and

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:35.120
charming person, let's go have adventures,
preferably involving ice cream!

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:38.160
“YES!”

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:44.380
With that in mind, it might be surprising
to learn that one of the show's most deep-cut

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:49.640
references comes from one of the very first
things we learn about the show: its setting.

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:56.220
It's easy to think that Kara Danvers' adopted
hometown of National City has a standard issue

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:59.960
generic name like most of the other fictional
towns in the DC Universe.

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:04.720
After all, Gotham is just an old nickname
for New York, and Metropolis is literally

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:06.800
just a word that means "big city."

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:11.560
Unlike those, however, National City doesn't
have its roots in comics.

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:16.240
It was created for the show, and its name
has a meaning that goes a little deeper than

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:20.940
just being a city within a nation, which many,
if not most, cities are.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:25.660
Instead, National City is a reference to Supergirl's
publisher.

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:28.780
No, not Cat Grant, but DC Comics.

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:35.220
The fact that "DC" stands for "Detective Comics"
is pretty well-known meaning that yes, "DC

00:01:35.229 --> 00:01:40.700
Comics" is actually "Detective Comics Comics"
but fewer people know that this wasn't actually

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:43.070
the company's official name until the late
'70s.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:47.620
Before that, they were National Periodical
Publications.

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:52.040
It's nice nod to the character's history,
since National was still the company's name

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:55.900
when Supergirl was introduced in 1959, but
it's pretty obscure.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:02.120
If only there was some major city they could've
used with some connection to the letters "D.C."

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:09.119
In "Shelter From the Storm," the 18th episode
of Supergirl's third season, Kara, Alex, Mon-El,

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:12.280
and Reign all take a trip to Lex Luthor's
mansion.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:17.400
As you might expect, his interior decorating
tastes mostly lean towards deathtraps meant

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:21.900
to kill Superman, but it's not all wall-mounted
machine guns and chunks of Kryptonite.

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:26.480
Once the big brawl starts up downstairs, we
get a pretty good look at his art collection

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:29.880
when Supergirl and Reign start knocking each
other through paintings.

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:35.710
Fortunately, Lex kept one important piece
of art upstairs, out of harm's way: Saint

00:02:35.710 --> 00:02:38.040
Michael Vanquishing Satan, by Raphael.

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:42.500
In real life, it's a priceless work of art
that hangs in the Louvre, and considering

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:47.319
that it's literally a painting of good triumphing
over evil, it seems like an odd choice for

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:48.020
Lex Luthor.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:53.700
There is, however, a reason for it to be there,
and it has nothing to do with Lex's taste

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:55.760
in art at least, not on the show.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:01.760
Instead, it's a callback to Batman v Superman:
Dawn of Justice, where Lex has a version of

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:04.140
the same scene hanging in his office.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:07.100
It is, however, a different piece of art.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:12.120
The movie used a more dramatic version by
Gustav Doré called "The Fall of the Rebel

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:17.299
Angels," which Lex hangs it upside down to
depict Satan descending from the sky in a

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:20.040
pretty un-subtle metaphor about his feelings
for Superman.

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:22.540
Still, it's a depiction of the same scene.

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:27.300
Turns out Lex's tastes are pretty consistent,
no matter what universe he's in.

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:32.120
Most people are familiar with Kryptonite,
and most comic book fans are aware that the

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:37.140
familiar Green K is only one of several types
that have shown up in comics through the years.

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:43.240
Supergirl has tapped into this long tradition,
too, digging up Blue, Red, and even "Synthetic"

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:45.960
Kryptonite, all of which have their origins
in the comics.

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:52.660
In the finale of season 2, however, the show
busts out a pretty obscure one: Silver Kryptonite,

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:57.360
which first appeared in 1963's Superman's
Pal Jimmy Olsen #70.

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:00.760
Well, except that it didn't, because it wasn't
actually real.

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:03.760
Well, even less real than all the other stuff.

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:07.920
In "The Secret of the Silver Kryptonite,"
Jimmy tricked Superman into being worried

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:13.620
about a strange new strain with unknown effects,
only for it to turn out as a hoax.

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:18.980
It was silver for Superman's silver anniversary
— the story was meant to mark 25 years since

00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:21.080
the Man of Steel's debut in 1938.

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:27.320
The version we see on Supergirl, however,
is very real, or at least as real as regular

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:31.100
green Kryptonite, and can cause paranoia and
hallucinations.

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:35.880
In Kara's case, that means that her cousin
Clark attacks her because he thinks she's

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:39.120
General Zod, but this version has a precedent,
too.

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:44.320
It's actually a reference to the CW's previous
long-running Super-show, Smallville, where

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:49.180
it showed up in season 5 with a similar effect
on that show's version of Clark Kent.

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:54.600
You'd expect Supergirl to have plenty of references
to the old Richard Donner and Christopher

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:58.360
Reeve Superman movies that made audiences
believe a man could fly.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:03.030
What you might not have expected was that
they'd build huge plot points around elements

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:06.240
lifted from the Supergirl movie that spun
off of them.

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:11.020
Which, at the time, convinced audiences to
stay at home instead of going to see it.

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:15.880
Kara's 1984 cinematic outing is often forgotten
by fans.

00:05:16.520 --> 00:05:21.800
Despite the fact that star Helen Slater made
a fantastic Supergirl in her own right, and

00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:26.870
would wind up playing Kara's adoptive mom
on the TV show, the movie was honestly not

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:27.780
very good.

00:05:28.550 --> 00:05:33.820
The inconsistent, confusing plot was largely
built around a Kryptonian McGuffin called

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:39.180
the Omegahedron, which had the vague power
to do... well, whatever the plot required,

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:39.680
really.

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:45.440
Chasing it down was what led Supergirl to
Earth, and the bad guys mainly wanted to use

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:50.080
it for evil magic spells, which, you know,
sure, whatever.

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:55.420
Cut to 35 years later, and the show brings
in the Omegahedron as a major plot device

00:05:55.420 --> 00:05:56.500
in its first season.

00:05:57.140 --> 00:06:02.220
Not only was it used by Non to rebuild Indigo,
making her an even more formidable threat,

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:06.819
it was also the power source for Myriad, the
mind-control device that Supergirl faced off

00:06:06.820 --> 00:06:09.360
against in the last two episodes of the first
season.

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:14.160
Like the movie, the show kept the limits of
the Omegahedron's abilities pretty vague,

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:20.060
but they did identify it as a Kryptonian power
source, explaining why Myriad was so powerful

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:21.960
that it could even affect Superman.

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:28.960
Supergirl is obviously chock full of ideas
and elements pulled from the Superman comics,

00:06:28.979 --> 00:06:33.639
and with Lynda Carter appearing as President
Marsdin, there are more than a few nods to

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:34.640
Wonder Woman, too.

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:40.080
As far as the rest of the DC Universe goes,
however, you might be surprised to learn that

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:44.420
the show's most prominent pull from Gotham
City has very little to do with the Dark Knight.

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:47.860
Instead, it comes from the pages of Hitman.

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:53.340
Created by Garth Ennis and John McCrea, Tommy
Monaghan originally appeared as part of the

00:06:53.349 --> 00:06:59.190
1993 Bloodlines event, in which every DC Comic
of the time introduced a brand new hero or

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:00.400
villain to the universe.

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:06.410
If you're wondering why you've never heard
of, say, Shadowstryke, Razorsharp, or Ballistic,

00:07:06.410 --> 00:07:10.670
it's because Tommy, a working-class hitman
for hire whose powers gave him a headache

00:07:10.670 --> 00:07:13.940
when he tried to use them, was the only one
who was any good.

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:19.300
For some reason, Gunfire, the man who could
turn anything into a gun, didn't click with

00:07:19.300 --> 00:07:19.800
audiences.

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:22.620
What does this have to do with Supergirl?

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:27.550
Well, Tommy was from a neighborhood in Gotham
City called the Cauldron, where his main hangout

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:29.020
was a bar called Noonan's.

00:07:29.449 --> 00:07:33.479
If you're a fan of Supergirl, you might recognize
that as the name of the restaurant where Kara

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:36.780
worked as a waitress before being hired by
Cat Grant.

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:42.539
The show's brightly lit, family friendly dining
establishment is a far cry from a dive bar

00:07:42.539 --> 00:07:48.149
frequented by hired killers with a literal
demon named Baytor as a bartender, but when

00:07:48.149 --> 00:07:52.410
it comes to naming places on a superhero show,
there's no such thing as coincidence.

00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:57.289
Incidentally, this one actually has a reference
within a reference: one of the most memorable

00:07:57.289 --> 00:08:02.260
scenes set in Supergirl's version of Noonan's
comes from the season 1 episode "Falling."

00:08:02.920 --> 00:08:08.330
When Kara is exposed to Red Kryptonite and
briefly becomes a jerk, she flicks peanuts

00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:11.800
at a shelf of bottles behind the bar, shattering
them.

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:17.639
If you're a super-cinephile, you'll recognize
that as a direct recreation of a scene from

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:18.960
Superman III.

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:24.460
One of the great things about a show like
Supergirl is that with so much time to work

00:08:24.460 --> 00:08:29.150
with over the course of several seasons and
so much history for one of the best characters

00:08:29.150 --> 00:08:34.120
in comics, the creators can really dig back
into the past and reintroduce forgotten elements

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:34.960
of a character.

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:37.780
Take, for instance, Rick Malverne.

00:08:38.060 --> 00:08:39.060
"Malverne.

00:08:39.070 --> 00:08:40.180
Why does that sound so familiar?"

00:08:40.180 --> 00:08:41.600
“Because he grew up in Midvale."

00:08:41.980 --> 00:08:46.540
Even on the show, he's not the most prominent
character; only one appearance outside of

00:08:46.540 --> 00:08:49.170
a flashback, in season 2's "Alex."

00:08:49.170 --> 00:08:53.300
There, he's depicted as a childhood friend
of Kara's who learned her secret back in their

00:08:53.300 --> 00:08:58.260
school days, but kept it to himself until
he was an adult, using that knowledge to kidnap

00:08:58.260 --> 00:09:02.240
Alex and manipulate Supergirl into breaking
his father out of prison.

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:07.320
Unless you've read a bunch of Supergirl comics
from 50 years ago, you might've missed that

00:09:07.340 --> 00:09:11.680
Rick is actually from the comics, although
he's a very different character there.

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:16.959
On the page, Dick Malverne, who got a name
change for obvious reasons, was Supergirl's

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:19.620
version of Lois Lane or Lana Lang.

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:23.860
He was the childhood friend who suspected
Supergirl's secret identity and kept trying

00:09:23.860 --> 00:09:28.980
to get her to reveal it, which in Dick's case
once escalated into literally faking his own

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:29.480
death.

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:34.760
Like his TV counterpart, it was later revealed
that Dick had always known Supergirl's identity

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:39.440
for a fact, and his elaborate plans were his
way of dunking on the girl he had a crush

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:39.940
on.

00:09:40.300 --> 00:09:42.980
Truly, a character who lives up to his name.

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:48.800
There are plenty of references to comics to
be found in dialogue throughout Supergirl,

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:52.940
but on the off chance that you haven't noticed,
there's also a reference in most of the episode

00:09:52.940 --> 00:09:54.300
titles throughout the series.

00:09:54.980 --> 00:10:00.510
In Season 2, we got one that was a nesting
doll of deep-cut comics references: "Supergirl

00:10:00.510 --> 00:10:01.500
Lives."

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:06.520
There was once a movie in development with
the same title, And yes: it's that Superman

00:10:06.520 --> 00:10:07.020
movie.

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:11.500
The one that Tim Burton was going to make,
with Nicolas Cage in a glorious mullet wig

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:15.200
and that amazingly weird Batman &amp; Robin-style
muscle suit.

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:18.700
All things considered, it's probably a good
thing that movie never happened.

00:10:19.220 --> 00:10:20.980
There's another layer to the reference, though.

00:10:20.980 --> 00:10:25.480
"Supergirl Lives" was directed by podcaster,
filmmaker, and occasional comic book writer

00:10:25.490 --> 00:10:30.540
Kevin Smith, who wrote the screenplay for
Superman Lives and has frequently spoken about

00:10:30.540 --> 00:10:31.060
the experience.

00:10:31.760 --> 00:10:36.110
There are even a couple of references to his
unproduced screenplay in the episode, if you're

00:10:36.110 --> 00:10:38.640
sharp and nerdy enough to catch them.

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:40.580
"It's Thanagarian snarebeast."

00:10:41.900 --> 00:10:46.320
It won't blow anybody's mind to learn that
most of the characters we see on Supergirl,

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:49.560
especially the ones who run around in costumes,
are from the comics.

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:50.380
Guardian?

00:10:50.780 --> 00:10:54.120
He's from the comics, although he's never
been Jimmy Olsen under the mask.

00:10:54.340 --> 00:10:55.740
Indigo, the evil robot?

00:10:56.000 --> 00:10:57.080
She's from the comics, too!

00:10:57.380 --> 00:10:58.620
The Martian Manhunter?

00:10:58.900 --> 00:11:04.060
That dude's been a member of the Justice League
since 1961, you know he's from the comics!

00:11:04.460 --> 00:11:06.760
Here's one that might actually surprise you,
though.

00:11:06.760 --> 00:11:11.660
Remember Zook, the imaginary friend that J'onn
mentions having when he was a kid back on

00:11:11.660 --> 00:11:12.160
Mars?

00:11:12.420 --> 00:11:13.860
"What was his name?"

00:11:17.240 --> 00:11:17.740
"Zook."

00:11:17.960 --> 00:11:20.360
Yeah, that is also from the comics.

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:21.580
No, really.

00:11:21.740 --> 00:11:26.200
In the Silver Age, there was a very weird
trend of superheroes having weird little magical

00:11:26.200 --> 00:11:29.060
imps showing up in their stories to cause
wacky troubles.

00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:33.840
Obviously, the most famous, and the one who
got the whole trend started, is Superman's

00:11:33.850 --> 00:11:39.450
fifth-dimensional foe Mr. Mxyzptlk, who appeared
on Supergirl in season 2, and you've probably

00:11:39.450 --> 00:11:42.240
heard of Bat-Mite, the Caped Crusader's biggest
fan.

00:11:43.100 --> 00:11:46.580
Those are just the well-known tips of the
inter-dimensional iceberg, though.

00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:52.060
Aquaman was annoyed by a sprite named Qwisp,
who had a twin brother named Quink.

00:11:52.060 --> 00:11:56.440
The Flash even had one, Mopee, a "guardian
angel" who was revealed to have been completely

00:11:56.440 --> 00:12:01.170
responsible for Barry Allen getting his powers
in a story that fans hated so much that it

00:12:01.170 --> 00:12:03.000
was immediately retconned out of existence.

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:08.660
Zook, on the other hand, managed to rack up
18 appearances in the Silver Age as a full-on

00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:13.980
sidekick before being shuffled off into the
limbo of things we don't talk about… unless

00:12:13.980 --> 00:12:14.980
it's on Supergirl.

00:12:15.520 --> 00:12:17.960
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:12:17.980 --> 00:12:21.320
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
stuff are coming soon.

00:12:21.680 --> 00:12:25.180
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

